<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hygelac</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 521</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Chlochilaicus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hygelac is the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a> in the Old English poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a>, the hero's uncle and lord. The poem describes his death on a raid into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>, an event that matches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>' account of a Scandinavian king, Chlochilaicus, killed by the Frankish prince Theudebert in the early 6th century. He is therefore one of the few figures of Beowulf generally thought to be historical.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hygelac.jpg" alt="Hygelac" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Fouquet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_entre_Francs_et_Danois_en_515.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
